0 was not a defined term. The term has now been defined in 3.4.5 of IEC 61508-4 ed2.0.</span></p></body>)/Rect[212.339 709.459 232.454 723.544]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj1977 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 1976 0 R/Rect[595.22 603.13 775.22 723.13]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj1978 0 obj<</AP<</N 8186 0 R>>/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/Contents(\(1\) The term "element" although used extensively in IEC 61508 ed1.0 was not a defined term. The term has now been defined in 3.4.5 of IEC 61508-4 ed2.0.)/CreationDate(D:20100523155808+02'00')/F 132/M(D:20100524172446+02'00')/NM(60ca8c7b-5084-4887-9b52-d6f574df371a)/P 1972 0 R/Popup 1979 0 R/QuadPoints[504.479 662.035 517.397 662.035 504.479 648.778 517.397 648.778]/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:7.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;font-weight:bold">\(1\)</span><span d\ir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt"> The term &quot;element&quot; although used extensively in IEC 61508 ed1.0 was not a defined term. The term has now been defined in 3.4.5 of IEC 61508-4 ed2.0.</span></p></body>)/Rect[500.94 648.364 520.936 662.449]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj1979 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 1978 0 R/Rect[595.22 542.035 775.22 662.035]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj1980 0 obj<</AP<</N 8190 0 R>>/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/Contents(\(86\) Systematic capabilityŽ is a new concept; see 3.5.9 of IEC 61508-4 ed2.0.)/CreationDate(D:20100523182157+02'00')/F 132/M(D:20100524172446+02'00')/NM(b215e61b-3345-451a-b8eb-f29a41e012d9)/P 1972 0 R/Popup 1981 0 R/QuadPoints[232.56 723.13 251.479 723.13 232.56 709.873 251.479 709.873]/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:7.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;font-weight:bold">\(86\)</span><span \dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt"> &#8220;Systematic capability&#8221; is a new concept; see 3.5.9 of IEC 61508-4 ed2.0.</span></p></body>)/Rect[229.021 709.459 255.018 723.544]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj1981 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 1980 0 R/Rect[595.22 603.13 775.22 723.13]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj1982 0 obj<</AP<</N 8179 0 R>>/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/Contents(\(87\) Subclause 7.4.4 of IEC 61508-2 ed2.0 hardware safety integrity architectural constraintsŽ has replaced 7.4.3.1 IEC 61508-2 ed1.0 architectural constraints on hardware safety integrityŽ. Whereas in ed1.0 there was only one route to achievement of \the required architectural constraints, ed2.0 has two routes. The hardware safety integrity constraints have to be achieved by implementing one of two possible routes. The two routes are:\rRoute 1H: based on hardware fault tolerance and safe failure fract\ion concepts, or\rRoute 2H: based on component reliability data from feedback from end users, increased confidence levels and hardware fault tolerance for specified safety integrity levels. The concept of Route 2H is new to IEC 61508 ed2.0.\rRoute 1H has \some significant differences of detail compared to the hardware fault tolerance and safe failure fraction concepts in ed1.0. In general it will not be helpful to compare subclauses of 7.4.3 of IEC 61508-1 ed1.0 with those of 7.4.4 of IEC 61508-2 ed2.0. Th\e only meaningful comparison is to assess the results obtained, in the context of a specific application, using 7.4.3 of IEC 61508-2 ed1.0 with those obtained using 7.4.4 of IEC 61508-2 ed2.0.)/CreationDate(D:20100523182251+02'00')/F 132/M(D:20100524172446+02'00')/NM(8b060a5d-e650-4b68-b89e-4fe68c9ab7ca)/P 1972 0 R/Popup 1983 0 R/QuadPoints[393.477 441.98 412.396 441.98 393.477 428.723 412.396 428.723]/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:7.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;font-weight:bold">\(87\)</span><span \dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt"> Subclause 7.4.4 of IEC 61508-2 ed2.0 &#8220;hardware safety integrity architectural constraints&#8221; has replaced 7.4.3.1 IEC 61508-2 ed1.0 &#8220;architectural constraints on hardware safety integrity&#8221;. Whereas\ in ed1.0 there was only one route to achievement of the required architectural constraints, ed2.0 has two routes. The hardware safety integrity constraints have to be achieved by implementing one of two possible routes. The two routes are:&#13;Route 1</s\pan><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;vertical-align:-5.0pt">H</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">: based on hardware fault tolerance and safe failure fraction concepts, or&#13;Route 2</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;vertic\al-align:-5.0pt">H</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">: based on component reliability data from feedback from end users, increased confidence levels and hardware fault tolerance for specified safety integrity levels. The concept of Route 2</s\pan><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;vertical-align:-5.0pt">H </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">is new to IEC 61508 ed2.0.</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;vertical-align:-5.0pt">&#13;</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-si\ze:10.0pt">Route 1</span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;vertical-align:-5.0pt">H </span><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">has some significant differences of detail compared to the hardware fault tolerance and safe failure fraction concept\s in ed1.0. In general it will not be helpful to compare subclauses of 7.4.3 of IEC 61508-1 ed1.0 with those of 7.4.4 of IEC 61508-2 ed2.0. The only meaningful comparison is to assess the results obtained, in the context of a specific application, using 7\.4.3 of IEC 61508-2 ed1.0 with those obtained using 7.4.4 of IEC 61508-2 ed2.0.</span></p></body>)/Rect[389.938 428.309 415.935 442.394]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj1983 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 1982 0 R/Rect[595.22 321.98 775.22 441.98]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj1984 0 obj<</Filter/FlateDecode/Length 7748>>stream
