<</AP<</N 9375 0 R>>/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/Contents(\(333\) This requirement has been introduced to make it clear that if a support tool includes a run-time component which is included in the safety related application, then that run-time component must be treated as a safety related element in accordance \with the requirements of the Standard. A common case is the run-time library provided by a programming language compiler, but other support tools such as those which generate code from a design representation could provide run-time elements.)/CreationDate(D:20100524142606+02'00')/F 132/M(D:20100524172819+02'00')/NM(d414734b-e31c-4e85-92c1-9f9bc6eae456)/P 3814 0 R/Popup 3824 0 R/QuadPoints[177.482 746.058 202.394 746.058 177.482 732.801 202.394 732.801]/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:7.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;font-weight:bold">\(333\) </span><spa\n dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">This requirement has been introduced to make it clear that if a support tool includes a run-time component which is included in the safety related application, then that run-time component must be treated as a safety r\elated element in accordance with the requirements of the Standard. A common case is the run-time library provided by a programming language compiler, but other support tools such as those which generate code from a design representation could provide run\-time elements.</span></p></body>)/Rect[173.943 732.386 205.933 746.472]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj3824 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 3823 0 R/Rect[595.22 626.058 775.22 746.058]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj3825 0 obj<</AP<</N 9385 0 R>>/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/Contents(\(334\) The requirements for the selection and validation of tools to support the development, verification and testing of safety related software have been greatly extended in ed2.0, to reflect the importance of this topic and the wide variety of support\ tools which are now available. Tools are classified as T1, T2, or T3 depending on their purpose in the software safety lifecycle and the possible effects of incorrect outputs from the tool \(note that these tool classes are defined in IEC 61508-4 rather \than in Part 3\). For example, a software design tool which provides automatic source code generation is more critical than a simple text editor, and a compilation system more critical than a source code generator. For tools with higher criticality \(T2 a\nd T3\), a specification of the tool is required so that its behaviour and the implications of using it can be fully understood.)/CreationDate(D:20100524142635+02'00')/F 132/M(D:20100524172819+02'00')/NM(b6b49d9f-e9bd-4194-a9a5-f3a7a11e934b)/P 3814 0 R/Popup 3826 0 R/QuadPoints[181.197 688.937 206.105 688.937 181.197 675.68 206.105 675.68]/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:7.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;font-weight:bold">\(334\) </span><spa\n dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">The requirements for the selection and validation of tools to support the development, verification and testing of safety related software have been greatly extended in ed2.0, to reflect the importance of this topic an\d the wide variety of support tools which are now available. Tools are classified as T1, T2, or T3 depending on their purpose in the software safety lifecycle and the possible effects of incorrect outputs from the tool \(note that these tool classes are d\efined in IEC 61508-4 rather than in Part 3\). For example, a software design tool which provides automatic source code generation is more critical than a simple text editor, and a compilation system more critical than a source code generator. For tools w\ith higher criticality \(T2 and T3\), a specification of the tool is required so that its behaviour and the implications of using it can be fully understood.</span></p></body>)/Rect[177.658 675.266 209.644 689.351]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj3826 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 3825 0 R/Rect[595.22 568.937 775.22 688.937]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj3827 0 obj<</AP<</N 9384 0 R>>/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/Contents(\(334\) The requirements for the selection and validation of tools to support the development, verification and testing of safety related software have been greatly extended in ed2.0, to reflect the importance of this topic and the wide variety of support\ tools which are now available. Tools are classified as T1, T2, or T3 depending on their purpose in the software safety lifecycle and the possible effects of incorrect outputs from the tool \(note that these tool classes are defined in IEC 61508-4 rather \than in Part 3\). For example, a software design tool which provides automatic source code generation is more critical than a simple text editor, and a compilation system more critical than a source code generator. For tools with higher criticality \(T2 a\nd T3\), a specification of the tool is required so that its behaviour and the implications of using it can be fully understood.)/CreationDate(D:20100524142646+02'00')/F 132/M(D:20100524172819+02'00')/NM(4459d17e-db02-459a-8b47-fe61b9b345f8)/P 3814 0 R/Popup 3828 0 R/QuadPoints[396.234 342.62 421.153 342.62 396.234 329.363 421.153 329.363]/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:7.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;font-weight:bold">\(334\) </span><spa\n dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">The requirements for the selection and validation of tools to support the development, verification and testing of safety related software have been greatly extended in ed2.0, to reflect the importance of this topic an\d the wide variety of support tools which are now available. Tools are classified as T1, T2, or T3 depending on their purpose in the software safety lifecycle and the possible effects of incorrect outputs from the tool \(note that these tool classes are d\efined in IEC 61508-4 rather than in Part 3\). For example, a software design tool which provides automatic source code generation is more critical than a simple text editor, and a compilation system more critical than a source code generator. For tools w\ith higher criticality \(T2 and T3\), a specification of the tool is required so that its behaviour and the implications of using it can be fully understood.</span></p></body>)/Rect[392.694 328.949 424.692 343.034]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj3828 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 3827 0 R/Rect[595.22 222.62 775.22 342.62]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj3829 0 obj<</AP<</N 9388 0 R>>/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/Contents(\(334\) The requirements for the selection and validation of tools to support the development, verification and testing of safety related software have been greatly extended in ed2.0, to reflect the importance of this topic and the wide variety of support\ tools which are now available. Tools are classified as T1, T2, or T3 depending on their purpose in the software safety lifecycle and the possible effects of incorrect outputs from the tool \(note that these tool classes are defined in IEC 61508-4 rather \than in Part 3\). For example, a software design tool which provides automatic source code generation is more critical than a simple text editor, and a compilation system more critical than a source code generator. For tools with higher criticality \(T2 a\nd T3\), a specification of the tool is required so that its behaviour and the implications of using it can be fully understood.)/CreationDate(D:20100524142650+02'00')/F 132/M(D:20100524172819+02'00')/NM(fa3182c3-0c21-454b-aeec-d2514f14bd21)/P 3814 0 R/Popup 3830 0 R/QuadPoints[369.352 281.406 394.271 281.406 369.352 268.149 394.271 268.149]/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:7.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;font-weight:bold">\(334\) </span><spa\n dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">The requirements for the selection and validation of tools to support the development, verification and testing of safety related software have been greatly extended in ed2.0, to reflect the importance of this topic an\d the wide variety of support tools which are now available. Tools are classified as T1, T2, or T3 depending on their purpose in the software safety lifecycle and the possible effects of incorrect outputs from the tool \(note that these tool classes are d\efined in IEC 61508-4 rather than in Part 3\). For example, a software design tool which provides automatic source code generation is more critical than a simple text editor, and a compilation system more critical than a source code generator. For tools w\ith higher criticality \(T2 and T3\), a specification of the tool is required so that its behaviour and the implications of using it can be fully understood.</span></p></body>)/Rect[365.812 267.735 397.81 281.82]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj3830 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 3829 0 R/Rect[595.22 161.406 775.22 281.406]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj3831 0 obj<</AP<</N 9381 0 R>>/C[1.0 1.0 0.0]/Contents(\(335\) The implications of using tools in classes T2 and T3 must be analysed and appropriate measures taken to mitigate tool failures \(the important failures are those which lead to the tools producing incorrect or misleading results, rather than simple\ crashesŽ\). Mitigations can include measures taken during development, such as inspection of the tool outputs, run-time defences, or both.)/CreationDate(D:20100524142718+02'00')/F 132/M(D:20100524172829+02'00')/NM(71a14db9-fa15-436d-955c-45d62bbd15c2)/P 3814 0 R/Popup 3832 0 R/QuadPoints[350.039 146.889 374.947 146.889 350.039 133.633 374.947 133.633]/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:7.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2" ><p dir="ltr"><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt;font-weight:bold">\(335\) </span><spa\n dir="ltr" style="font-size:10.0pt">The implications of using tools in classes T2 and T3 must be analysed and appropriate measures taken to mitigate tool failures \(the important failures are those which lead to the tools producing incorrect or misleadin\g results, rather than simple &#8220;crashes&#8221;\). Mitigations can include measures taken during development, such as inspection of the tool outputs, run-time defences, or both.</span></p></body>)/Rect[346.5 133.218 378.486 147.304]/Subtype/Highlight/Type/Annot>>endobj3832 0 obj<</F 28/Open false/Parent 3831 0 R/Rect[595.22 26.8894 775.22 146.889]/Subtype/Popup/Type/Annot>>endobj3833 0 obj<</Filter/FlateDecode/Length 5911>>stream
